# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Board overlay: ESP32-S3 N16R8 (16 MB flash, 8 MB octal PSRAM).
# Overlays the root sdkconfig.defaults and wins on conflicts (composed in the
# root CMakeLists.txt).
#
# The chip itself is NOT set here. IDF resolves IDF_TARGET in the same early
# pass that cannot see -DBOARD (the note in main/CMakeLists.txt), so a
# CONFIG_IDF_TARGET line in this file is read too late and silently loses to
# whatever the existing sdkconfig says. Select the chip the ordinary way:
#   idf.py -DBOARD=esp32s3_n16r8 set-target esp32s3
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Flash size and the partition table that fits it. This board is the reason
# both moved out of the root defaults: the root asserted 4 MB, the overlay
# wins on conflicts, and the drift guard then correctly refused a build whose
# generated sdkconfig disagreed with an assertion it had been handed. The
# assertion was in the wrong file, not wrong.
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_16MB=y
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM_FILENAME="partitions-16mb.csv"

# ── PSRAM ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 8 MB of it, in OCTAL mode: the R8 half of N16R8 is an octal part, and the
# IDF default is quad. Getting this wrong does not fail the build - PSRAM
# simply is not found at boot, the log says so once, and everything that
# wanted it silently falls back to internal RAM. ConsoleManager's 40 KB log
# ring is already written that way (heap_caps_calloc with MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM,
# then plain calloc), so nothing breaks loudly.
#
# 80 MHz rather than 120: 120 MHz is behind IDF_EXPERIMENTAL_FEATURES on the
# S3 and carries timing caveats. 80 MHz is the speed the module is specified
# for and is not a bottleneck for anything here.
#
# VERIFIED ON HARDWARE 2026-09-18, and it needed to be: this is the one setting
# here that a green build says nothing about. Quad instead of octal, or a module
# that is not really an R8, boots fine and simply finds less PSRAM, or none.
# What the S3 (MAC 80:b5:4e:db:47:18) actually reported at boot:
#
#   octal_psram: vendor id : 0x0d (AP)     density : 0x03 (64 Mbit)
#   esp_psram:   Found 8MB PSRAM device    Speed: 80MHz
#   esp_psram:   SPI SRAM memory test OK
#   esp_psram:   Adding pool of 8192K of PSRAM memory to heap allocator
#
# The memory test is the part that matters - it is the difference between the
# chip answering and the bus actually working at this speed.
CONFIG_SPIRAM=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_OCT=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_SPEED_80M=y
